{"availability":"available","full":"frostyard/nsl","name":"nsl","description":"NSpawn Subsystem for Linux: development environments for atomic hosts and people who want to keep the host clean","category":"Developer tools","language":"Go","stars":98,"forks":5,"created":"2026-09-26T20:08:28Z","updated":"2026-10-10T23:15:46Z","license":"MIT","branch":"main","homepage":"https://frostyard.github.io/nsl/","topics":["developer-tools","nspawn","vm","vmspawn","wsl"],"demo":"https://frostyard.github.io/nsl/","color":"#323057","ink":"#d6caff","lastCheckedAt":"2026-10-12T13:37:57.605Z","lastAttemptAt":"2026-10-12T13:37:57.605Z","lastAvailableAt":"2026-10-12T13:37:57.605Z","metrics":[{"at":"2026-10-09T03:37:56.110Z","stars":98,"forks":5},{"at":"2026-10-12T13:37:57.605Z","stars":98,"forks":5}],"technologies":["Go"],"overview":{"kind":"readme","version":1,"paragraphs":["Leave the host alone. Work in another distro.","Keep your development tools separate from your Linux host. You may need somewhere to apt install a project's dependencies, try a toolchain packaged for another distro, or run a service. nsl gives you persistent Linux machines for that work, as WSL does on Windows. Each machine is a whole distro with its own packages and services. It opens in the directory you were in, works on your files, and stops when you stop using it.","Create your first machine walks through creation, a login shell, running a command and stopping the VM."],"features":[],"sourceUrl":"https://github.com/frostyard/nsl/blob/main/README.md","fingerprint":"1e42b282f2dc892cf6606e7a22061d18f2317edc2b8ee2b136d055f17f151273","extractedAt":"2026-10-12T13:37:57.787Z"},"overviewCheckedAt":"2026-10-12T13:37:57.787Z","projectInsights":{"version":1,"whatItDoes":{"text":"Leave the host alone. Work in another distro.","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/frostyard/nsl/blob/main/README.md"},"audience":[],"requirements":[{"kind":"account","status":"required","text":"Create your first machine walks through creation, a log